TI  - MANAGING PAEDIATRIC URTICARIA AND ANGIOEDEMA IN THE EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT
AU  - de Waal, P.
PY  - 2024
PD  - 
N2  - Urticaria and angioedema are common in children who present to the Emergency Department. Given the broad range of possible diagnoses and the lack of immediate results from specialised tests in an acute setting, diagnosis depends largely on a thorough clinical history and careful physical examination. These patients may also present with life-threatening airway compromise or anaphylaxis, requiring immediate intervention and pharmacological treatment. Fortunately, in most cases, acute urticaria in children – though distressing to parents – is typically triggered by an infection and requires symptomatic treatment and reassurance. On the other hand, the cause of chronic urticaria often remains unknown. Angioedema may occur on its own and can be hereditary. In such cases, antihistamines and systemic corticosteroids are ineffective and treatment with fresh frozen plasma or newer on-demand therapies is recommended. If uncertain about the diagnosis, choice of special investigations or treatment, emergency physicians should promptly consult a specialist to ensure optimal management and follow-up care.
