N2  - Septic shock is characterized by an inability of the host to maintain vascular integrity and fluid homeostasis, resulting in inadequate tissue oxygenation and circulatory failure. Sepsis is a clinical syndrome with physiologic, biologic, and biochemical abnormalities caused by a dysregulated inflammatory response to infection. Screening tools in sepsis are utilized to appropriately identify patients at risk or diagnosed with sepsis; allocate appropriate resources such as intravenous fluid resuscitation, culture data, and timely antibiotics; and risk stratify to higher levels of care including intensive care unit and transfer to other centers. The clinical presentation of shock varies with the type and cause, but several features are common. These include hypotension, cool clammy skin and oliguria, changes in mental status, and metabolic acidosis. Physiologic adaptations to pregnancy that promote favorable maternal and fetal outcomes occur in almost every organ system. Early diagnosis of septic shock is critical to improve outcome. © 2024 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
