TI  - Airway Management in Intensive Care Medicine
AU  - Higgs, A.
AU  - Mosier, J.M.
PY  - 2022
PD  - 
N2  - Airway management in intensive care unit (ICU) patients is extremely demanding. Airway-related mortality and severe morbidity in the ICU are orders of magnitude higher than in general operating room practice. Intubation must be expeditious and smooth, as critically ill patients do not tolerate repeated, prolonged, or ill-prepared attempts. Some patients are so fragile that even optimal airway management places them at risk of severe complications, including cardiac arrest. Thorough preoxygenation and perioxygenation, competent use of video-assisted laryngoscopy, and utilization of an intubation checklist incorporating rapid sequence intubation are key management strategies. Awake intubation for apnea-intolerant patients and skill with flexible scope intubation techniques are also critical for improving safety. After intubation, an intubated patient care bundle including capnography should be followed. Partially displaced tubes present diagnostic uncertainty, and the accompanying delay is harmful; the key to successful management is team-based training, well-formulated airway management strategies that are shared with the entire team, using equipment with which team members are proficient, and navigating through plans efficiently. When patients recover from their critical illness, difficult extubation must be assessed and planned for. Exchange catheters, rescue oxygenation techniques, such as supraglottic airway devices, and tracheostomy are all skills that airway managers in the ICU need to possess. Tracheostomy insertion is unique to ICU airway management, but more challenging still is to ensure that tracheostomy patients remain safe after the stoma is created; thus, confidence in dealing with tracheostomy emergencies is a core ICU skill. Another essential element of the intensivist’s airway repertoire is the management of patients with acutely inflamed airways and the ability to recognize the patterns of airway deterioration to which these patients are susceptible. Timely intervention requires constant vigilance, experience, shrewd judgment, and technical skill. It is incumbent on those who train intensivists to ensure their fellows are equipped to provide these vulnerable patients with the best possible care, keeping in mind that the emphasis should not be on devices but on training, good planning, and teamwork. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
