TI  - Wheezing in Older Children: Asthma
AU  - McDowell, K.M.
AU  - Mokhallati, N.
PY  - 2023
PD  - 
N2  - Asthma is the most common cause of wheezing in children and adolescents. Asthma is a complex, heterogeneous disorder with numerous phenotypes, which share common features of airway inflammation, hyperreactivity, and reversible obstruction. The pathophysiology, genetics, natural history, and response to treatment vary widely among phenotypes. Characteristic symptoms-wheezing, cough, chest tightness-may be precipitated by various triggers including allergens, exercise, infection, weather change, and emotion. Diagnosis is based on history, symptoms, demonstration of type 2 inflammation (in most instances), and documentation of reversible air flow obstruction. Disease management is focused on control rather than severity, with the primary goals of controlling symptoms while minimizing risks. Controller medications include anti-inflammatory agents, long-acting beta-agonists, and long-acting muscarinic antagonists. Reliever medications, used to alleviate symptoms and abort exacerbations, include rapid onset beta-agonists (e.g., albuterol), ipratropium or inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)-formoterol combinations. Recent guidelines emphasize the use of combination therapy (ICS when using beta-agonists) to treat symptoms. An effective patient-physician partnership is critical to successful asthma management as is a periodic review of symptoms, medication technique, and lung function, in conjunction with an assessment of modifiable risks and co-morbidities. Asthma exacerbations in children may be mild and respond quickly to reliever medications but can also rapidly progress into a medical emergency. Any asthma exacerbation that fails to respond to home management should be assessed and treated by medical personnel in the Emergency Department. Short-acting beta-agonists and systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay of therapy for acute episodes. Severe exacerbations should be treated in a hospital setting with beta-agonists, systemic steroids, intravenous magnesium sulfate, supplemental oxygen, subcutaneous epinephrine, and, in extreme cases, mechanical ventilation. Any exacerbation requiring admission is a sentinel event and should prompt a thorough review of the child's asthma management because it is the most significant risk factor for future attacks.. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
