N2  - Timing, strategies, and execution are important to avoid complications after tracheal extubation. Generally, optimal timing coincides with the patient being able to maintain adequate oxygenation, spontaneous ventilation, and a patent airway. Consciousness and restoration of reflexes should permit protection against aspiration. Prediction of complications has two components: the probability that a patient can remain extubated and, should this fail, the likelihood that a required reintubation can be achieved without additional complications. Examples of patients associated with increased risk of extubation failure include those with reduced respiratory reserve or a compromised airway. Airway compromise may be due to intrinsic swelling, laryngeal dysfunction, or extrinsic compression. Reintubation difficulty should be expected in patients who had been difficult to intubate under controlled conditions. However, previous ease of intubation does not ensure that an emergent reintubation will also be easy. Anatomic changes and the emergent nature of reintubation may result in limited availability of equipment and personnel leading to very difficult conditions. Extubation strategies aimed at minimizing the physiologic disturbance include deep extubation, low-dose opioids, local anesthetics, and the substitution of a supraglottic airway for the existing endotracheal tube (ETT). An airway exchange device (combined with indirect laryngoscopy) should be considered when reintubation might be more difficult to achieve. Communication of the difficulties encountered and the proposed extubation strategy is particularly important in mitigating extubation complications. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
