AU  - Sonny, A.
AU  - Pino, R.M.
PY  - 2024
PD  - 
N2  - An understanding of kidney physiology is essential for the anesthesiologist to prevent or minimize the degree of renal dysfunction in the perioperative period. The kidney is a complex organ that exemplifies the complex structure-function relationship that regulates intravascular volume, osmolality, acid-base and electrolyte balance, and the excretion of end products of metabolism and drugs. It also produces hormones involved with fluid balance, bone metabolism, and hematopoiesis. These multiple functions are often altered in patients with a variety of systemic diseases as well as in the perioperative period even in healthy patients. Afferent and efferent arteriolar tone is balanced by catecholamines, angiotensin II, and arginine vasopressin to stabilize glomerular filtration pressure. Molecules that pass through the size- and charge-selective barrier of the glomerular basement membrane enter the renal tubular system where autoregulation through tubuloglomerular feedback regulates solute and water concentrations during fluctuations in blood pressure. Most of the water and sodium of the daily 180 L protein-free glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed through a countercurrent mechanism, urea recycling, concentration, and dilution in the loop of Henle, and hormonal regulation of water permeability in the distal tubule and collecting ducts. The mediators of the sympathoadrenal axis, renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and vasopressin respond to hypotension and hypovolemia through vasoconstriction and retention of salt and water. Prostaglandins and natriuretic peptides facilitate vasodilation and the excretion of salt and water during periods of hypervolemia and hypertension. As a result, plasma osmolality is tightly controlled. Serum creatinine, a key monitor of perioperative renal function, reflects the balance between creatinine production from muscle and creatinine excretion by the kidney. As a result, it is a useful marker of the glomerular filtration rate. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
