TI  - Pathophysiology of the Upper and Lower Airways
AU  - Öztürk, B.Ç.
AU  - Gemicioğlu, B.
PY  - 2023
PD  - 
N2  - The pathophysiological alterations witnessed in rhinitis mostly affect two elements: the blood vessels and the glands. In the former e, they lead to congested sinuses, transudation, and swelling. In the latter e, glandular activity produces excess mucus. The result of both processes is nasal blockage. When allergens are bound by immunoglobulins, inflammation is provoked by signal release, causing nasal dysfunction to occur in several ways. Firstly, immune modulators (histamine, bradykinin, and the leukotrienes) have a direct action on the vasculature and glands within the submucosa, making the mucosa grow thicker and increase secretory activity. These modulators also activate nasal sensory nerve receptors, producing a reflex at the axonal level that triggers neuropeptide release. The release of neurokinins, among which substance P is notable, causes the vessels to dilate further and transudation to occur. Potentially neurokinins also influence glandular activity in the submucosa. Finally, the nasal sensory receptors also respond to stimulation through CNS reflexes, causing sneeze and irritation of the lining of the nose to occur, which are cardinal elements in rhinitis symptomatology, as well as contributing yet further to dilation of blood vessels in the nose, increasing mucus production and provoking reaction in the lower portions of the airway. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2023.
