N2  - Acute supraglottic edema can rapidly progress to airway obstruction and awake fibreoptic intubation is often considered first-line approach. We report a case of life-threatening supraglottic swelling where awake fibreoptic intubation failed due to marked edema and airway reactivity. Awake videolaryngoscopy enabled successful tracheal intubation, securing the airway while preserving spontaneous ventilation. This case highlights the importance of flexibility in airway strategy selection, the value of videolaryngoscopy as a rescue technique and the essential role of multidisciplinary coordination in upper airway emergencies. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
