TI  - Heliox therapy in tracheostomy
AU  - Apaydin, S.M.K.
PY  - 2025
PD  - 
N2  - Heliox is a respiratory gas consisting of a mixture of helium and oxygen. It can be used as an adjunct in the treatment of severe airway obstruction due to its high CO2 diffusion coefficient and fluidity. While it has no direct therapeutic effect, it serves as a temporary palliative measure until more definitive treatments take effect. Tracheostomy is indicated in intubated patients who are being monitored in the following situations:: inability to develop a weaning plan, failure of extubation, difficulty in weaning and prolonged dependency on respiratory support. In these cases, performing a tracheostomy is preferred between days 2 and 10. The optimal timing is controversial; however, recent studies have shown that performing a tracheostomy earlier than 14 dayscan shorten the length of stay in the intensive care unit. The reasons for performing an awake tracheostomy due to airway obstruction can be benign or malignant diseases. Benign conditions requiring awake tracheostomy include benign laryngeal tumors, subglottic stenosis, inflammatory conditions (such as deep neck infection and angioedema), bilateral vocal cord paralysis, maxillofacial trauma and burns. Either surgical or percutaneous tracheostomy methods can be used. The appropriate method should be determined according to hospital resources and the experience of the team planning the procedure. © 2025 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
