N2  - Allergic rhinitis is characterized by the symptoms of nasal itch, sneezing, rhinorrhea, and nasal stuffiness with, in more severe and chronic disease, the development of mucosal edema resulting in impaired sinus drainage, loss of sense of smell, and alteration in eustachian tube function. 1 These symptoms and disruption of normal function are consequent upon the local release of mediators from activated cells within the nasal mucosa, through interaction with end-organ receptors. In this respect the nose differs from the lower respiratory tract in that symptom expression and disordered function are secondary to alterations in neural and vascular components and does not involve airway smooth muscle. © 1994 by Taylor and Francis Group, LLC.
