TI  - Anesthesia for Otolaryngologic and Head-Neck Surgery
AU  - Patel, A.
PY  - 2024
PD  - 
N2  - Anesthesia for otolaryngologic and head-neck surgery requires close coordination between the anesthesiologist and surgeon to ensure uniform understanding of how best to address clinical issues, particularly those related to management of the shared airway problems and potential airway management challenges. A thorough preoperative evaluation should be completed including history to assess for evidence of obstructive sleep apnea, snoring, history of prolonged tobacco and alcohol use, and any previous surgery or radiation therapy to the head or neck. A preoperative endoscopic airway examination (PEAE) in selected patients may be required to determine if there is evidence of laryngeal disorders or supraglottic pathology. All available CT and MRI scans obtained preoperatively should be reviewed to further assess for evidence of any abnormalities that might precipitate airway obstruction or complicate tracheal intubation. For those patients for whom the airway management is anticipated to be challenging, awake tracheal intubation (ATI) using flexible bronchoscopy or video laryngoscopy may be required. In selected patients the airway may be so distorted that awake tracheal intubation is impractical and a tracheostomy under local anesthetic may be the only option for airway management. Shared airway procedures involving surgery of the glottis, subglottis, and trachea require an understanding of the special equipment and techniques used during surgery; for patients requiring laser surgical procedures, the anesthesiologist must be knowledgeable about laser safety. Many procedures require placement of oropharyngeal and/or nasopharyngeal throat packs. The packs must be gently positioned and, at the end of the procedures, must be accounted for and removed. In addition, the oral cavity must be suctioned to clear any secretions, blood, clots, or debris prior to tracheal extubation to reduce the risk of aspiration on emergence. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
