N2  - The current study looked at people who were at high risk of morbidity and mortality as a result of SARS-CoV-2 infection and who improved or had a moderate clinical course after taking doxycycline. Despite its safety, we would not recommend using doxycycline in the treatment of COVID-19+ people unless they are under the care and supervision of a physician. The benefits of ivermectin and doxycycline as potential COVID-19 treatments are now being evaluated by the Indian Council of Medical Research. Once fibrosis has developed, azithromycin may have antifibrotic and proapoptotic effects on primary fibroblasts. As a result, in the late fibroproliferative-fibrotic phase of ARDS (Acute respiratory distress syndrome), COVID-19, azithromycin may reduce lung fibrosis. Intranasally administered N-acetylcysteine has been shown to reduce neutrophils, lymphocytes, goblet cells, bacterial count, turbinate hypertrophy, nasal symptoms, and rhinorrhea in non-allergic chronic rhinitis with goblet cell metaplasia. N-mucolytic acetylcysteine's action, ability to restore surface ciliary activity at the nasal epithelium, and regulation of the inflammatory response, which is a crucial component of the nasal mucosa's immunological defense system, are all credited with the advantages. In acute recurrent rhinosinusitis, a nasal douche containing flunisolide and N-acetylcysteine has been linked to a less stuffy nose, decreased severity of symptoms and rhino sinus signs as measured by endoscopy, improved cytological grading of neutrophil and eosinophil cells, fewer exacerbations, and increased mucociliary motility. Montelukast, a cysteinyl leukotriene receptor antagonist, may be utilized to treat COVID-19 infection, and the possibility of slowing the progression of severe COVID-19 will be examined. This cutting-edge therapy method requires input from the COVID-19 community in order to improve it, such as better protecting our patients who are already vulnerable owing to corona. While information has flowed freely throughout the pandemic, results from ongoing and future clinical trials are still needed to better understand the interactions between different medicinal medicines in the context of COVID-19 infections. © 2024 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
