TI  - Complications of Managing the Airway
AU  - Schiff, J.-H.
AU  - Walther, A.
AU  - Krier, C.
AU  - Hagberg, C.A.
PY  - 2022
PD  - 
N2  - Airway management in anesthesia practice is often uncomplicated; however, some complications are dramatic and immediately life-threatening—some are severe and long-lasting or painful for the patient. Good clinical practice in all areas aims to avoid all these complications. In anesthesia practice, errors of omission are more common than errors of commission. Errors of omission include failure to recognize the magnitude of a problem, make appropriate observations, or act in a timely manner. Errors of commission include any trauma during the act of anesthetizing the patient. The most frequent cause of fatal errors in medical practice, especially in the field of airway management, is to ignore inadequate experience and skills and not call for help. The primary goal of anesthesiologists is to ensure the safety and well-being of their patients, and they are usually careful in performing the technical aspects of their jobs. Yet, each device has its own possible complications, therefore many potential errors can occur that may lead to harm to patients while securing the airway. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
