TI  - The Immunobiology of Asthma
AU  - Martin, R.A.
AU  - Poynter, M.E.
PY  - 2016
PD  - 
N2  - Asthma is a chronic syndrome affecting the respiratory system that is characterized by episodes of airflow obstruction and bronchial hyperresponsiveness, oftentimes accompanied by inflammation. Allergic asthma is a form of this syndrome in which the adaptive immune system recognizes a foreign antigen (termed an allergen), setting into motion a series of molecular and cellular events that culminate in pathology. For allergic sensitization to occur, the allergen must surmount innate barrier defenses: surfactant, mucous, and the epithelial cell layer. Epithelial cells actively partake in the inflammatory process by recognizing danger-associated molecular patterns with pattern recognition receptors, notably Toll-like receptors (TLRs). TLR signaling activates airway epithelial cells to produce inflammatory mediators, which then recruit antigen-presenting cells. In allergic airway disease, dendritic cells are critical in activating the adaptive immune system, classically generating a Th2 response. Th2 cytokines - IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 - promote IgE production, eosinophil recruitment, mucous production, and airway hyperresponsiveness. In clinical asthma, however, only 50% of patients demonstrate Th2 markers. The Th17 response is a distinct pathologic mechanism and biomarker for neutrophilic, glucocorticoid-resistant asthma. Although typically treated with bronchodilators and anti-inflammatories, disease heterogeneity remains a challenge in the clinical management of asthma. © 2016 Elsevier Ltd All rights reserved.
