N2  - Many neuropeptides are localized to sensory, parasympathetic, and sympathetic neurons in the respiratory tract (Table 1) (Uddman et al., 1993; Barnes et ah, 1991). These peptides have potent effects on bronchomotor tone, airway secretions, the bronchial circulation, and inflammatory and immune cells. Although the precise physiological roles of each peptide are not yet understood, some clues are provided by their localization and functional effects. Many of the inflammatory and functional effects of neuropeptides are relevant to asthma, and there is compelling evidence for the involvement of neuropeptides in the pathophysiology and symptomatology of asthma (Barnes, 1991a). The purpose of this chapter is to discuss effects of airway neuropeptides that are relevant to the pathophysiology of asthma and whether this might lead to new therapeutic approaches in the future. Table 1 Neuropeptides in the Respiratory Tract Peptide Localization Vasoactive intestinal peptide Parasympathetic (afferent) Peptide histidine isoleucine/methionine Peptide histidine valine-42 Helodermin PACAP-27 Galanin Substance P Afferent Neurokinin A Neuropeptide K Calcitonin gene-related peptide Gastrin-releasing peptide Neuropeptide Y Sympathetic Somatostatin Afferent/uncertain Enkephalin Cholecystokinin octapeptide. © 1994 by Marcel Dekker, Inc. All Rights Reserved.
