N2  - We experienced a case of significant angioedema caused by administration of renin-angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor (ACE-I) in a patient with severe heart failure. A 54-year-old-man was taken by ambulance to our hospitaL He developed bronchitis 2 days previously, and gradually became with less body movement Echocardiography showed that a left ventricular ejection fraction (EF) decreased to 10%, and no obvious coronary artery stenosis. The result of the biopsy suggested that heart failure was due to dilated cardiomyopathy. We administered ACE-I recommended to use for dilated cardiomyopathy, and then angioedema appeared at both side of his lips. Although he had been intubated already, angioedema is a serious adverse event of ACE-I; some patients required tracheal intubation or tracheostomy because of the upper airway stenosis. After we changed to the angiotensin II type receptor blocker (ARB), angioedema promptly disappeared. Although administration of ARB in patients with ACE-I induced angioedema is controversial we administered ARB to this patient after explanation to his family about risk and benefit of medica-tioa. © Kokuseido Publishing Co. Ltd. All rights reserved.
