N2  - Angio-oedema is a type of skin and soft tissue oedema generated by vasodilation secondary to an increased vascular permeability that may or may not be associated with alterations of complement, as well as coagulation factors. Genetic predisposition, a trigger and a mediator (histamine or bradykinin being the most frequently involved) are sufficient to cause this condition of potential severity. Recent advances in clinical research have led to the identification of new therapeutic targets, allowing for an effective treatment of most angio-oedema symptoms if administered on time. In this work a review is presented of the latest advances in angio-oedema in critically ill patients. © 2019
