TI  - Objective Assessment of Nasal Function
AU  - Ottaviano, G.
AU  - Van Gerven, L.
PY  - 2020
PD  - 
N2  - Nasal obstruction is defined as the subjective perception of discomfort or difficulty in the passage of air through the nostrils. It is a common complaint in many nasal conditions and affects the quality of life and the quality of sleep. Medical history and physical examination are key for diagnosing the specific cause. Objective measures can be used to diagnose and treat nasal obstruction. Mucociliary transport time can be used to study ciliary dysfunction either due to primary ciliary dyskinesia or just to inflammation. Peak nasal inspiratory flow (PNIF), acoustic rhinometry, and rhinomanometry, with their specific characteristics, assess different aspects of nasal obstruction. These methods roughly correlate with each other and all of them can be alternatively utilized in clinical practice, especially to evaluate the results of medical and surgical treatments. The choice of what method to use depends on the situation. It has been suggested that PNIF, being inexpensive, fast, portable, and simple, should be available in every practice where “airway” diseases are diagnosed and treated. As in the lower airways, objective and subjective evaluation gives different information that together can optimize the diagnosis and the choice of treatment. So, objective methods for the measurement of nasal obstruction are desirable, as there is an increasing need for evidence-based medicine. © 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
