TI  - General Considerations of Anesthesia and Management of the Difficult Airway
AU  - Mark, L.J.
AU  - Hillel, A.T.
AU  - Lester, L.
AU  - Akst, S.A.
AU  - Cover, R.
AU  - Herzer, K.
PY  - 2020
PD  - 
N2  - Airway management is the essence of clinical anesthesiology and otolaryngology. The goal of airway management is simple: to provide the most expeditious form of management that has the lowest potential for injury and the greatest potential for control of the airway. The approach to the patient with a difficult airway varies, depending on whether airway management is elective or urgent and whether the health care setting is an operating room (OR) or outside of an OR setting. This chapter first presents the anesthetic component of basic airway management in the OR, including the use of anesthetic drugs and their advantages and disadvantages, in a manner that otolaryngology-head and neck (OLHN) surgeons can incorporate into their practices. The latter half of the chapter describes the role of OLHN surgeons in the multidisciplinary Johns Hopkins Hospital Difficult Airway Response Team (DART) Program. Lessons learned from the DART program are briefly discussed. The chapter concludes with case examples from pre-DART, post-DART, and catastrophic events that OLHN surgeons might encounter. © 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
