TI  - Uvular angioedema with a rare etiology: A case report
AU  - Dermendzhiev, S.
AU  - Kalchev, Y.
AU  - Dermendzhiev, T.
PY  - 2018
PD  - 
N2  - Background: Isolated uvular edema is considered rare in clinical practice. Sometimes the edema is so severe and spreads so quickly that it puts the patient's life in danger due to airway obstruction. Knowledge of the etiology and pathogenesis is therefore of great importance for accurate and timely diagnosis and also a key factor for effective treatment. Material and Methods: We present a 40-year-old male patient who reported discomfort and a stinging sensation in his throat, several days after he had started to work in a phytocosmetics facility. The patient did not report any systemic allergic reactions or substance abuse and there were no history of existing food, medication or insect allergies as well as family history of immune-allergic reactions. Results: Medical examination revealed no evidence of laryngeal edema but an evident edema of the uvula was seen. After a thorough analysis of the chemicals included in the epicutaneous allergy test, we found that the patient was occupational^ exposed to the most of them. After a 5-day course of treatment with systemic corticosteroids and H1 and H2 blockers all of the symptoms gradually regressed and the edema was completely cleared. Upon discharge the patient was advised to change his working environment. Conclusions: This type of edema can be seen in people exposed to the compounds involved in cosmetics manufacturing. Occupational history and epicutaneous testing play leading roles in the diagnosis of angioneurotic edema resulting from materials used in cosmetic products. © 2018 Medical Information Center. All rights reserved.
