N2  - Epigenetic changes provide a mechanism whereby environmental insults leave a signature on the airways which determine the response to future stimuli thereby shaping the development of chronic inflammation and airway remodeling. There are three primary epigenetic processes: histone modifications, DNA modification, and non-coding RNAs. In this chapter we review the literature suggesting that epigenetic changes regulate a number of functions in airway cells in asthma using airway smooth muscle as an exemplar and focussing mostly on histone modifications as these have been more widely studied than other epigenetic modifications. We also discuss the therapeutic implications. © 2022 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved
