N2  - Tongue enlargement occurs at varying intensities and often requires direct intervention due to the side effects that it causes. One approach is to perform surgery to reduce the volume of the tongue. Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a prevalent disorder that frequently reduces or provisionally interrupts respiration during sleep. The surgical options can also be used to treat OSA. Some of the main complications associated with surgical treatment of these abnormalities include bleeding, edema, airway obstruction, wound dehiscence, nerve injury, paresthesia in the middle of the tongue, loss of taste, motor dysfunction, scarring, speech problems, structural deformity, change in muscular balance, recurrent macroglossia, and salivary duct injury. In this chapter, while addressing the concepts associated with macroglossia and OSA, we will investigate the complications of surgical treatments of these abnormalities and provide suggestions for the prevention and management of them. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2021.
