N2  - A 69-year-old man with a past history of hypertension on angiotensin II receptor blocker(ARB)for three months presented for radical prostatectomy. Immediately after induction of anesthesia with fentanyl and propofol, mask ventilation became difficult, although no significant hemodynamic changes occurred. Fiberoptic examination revealed severe oropharyngeal edema, but, the trachea was successfully intubated. Afterward, the operation proceeded without complications. He stayed in the ICU for 4 days until the trachea was extubated successfully. He was diagnosed with DIAE because of his history of dyspnea with exclusion of other possible pathophysiological conditions.
