AU  - Soo Hoo, G.W.
AU  - Lin, H.K.
AU  - Junaid, I.
AU  - Klaustermeyer, W.B.
PY  - 2015
PD  - 
N2  - Objective The purpose of this study was to review consecutive cases of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor angioedema admitted to an intensive care unit. Methods Fifty subjects with ACE-inhibitor angioedema admitted from 1998-2011 were reviewed. Results All 50 subjects were men, 62.8 ± 8.4 years of age, 76% African Americans. Fifteen (30%) required ventilatory support and 2 (4%) required tracheostomy. Over half (56%) had taken ACE inhibitors for over a year. Logistic regression identified dyspnea and tongue involvement with the need for ventilatory support (P <.01). Hypercapnia (PaCO2 = 45.2 ± 6.7; P = 0.046) also identified patients needing ventilatory support. Conclusions Ventilatory support was provided for about one-third of those with ACE inhibitor-associated angioedema. Angioedema can occur even after extended use. Dyspnea and tongue involvement identified patients requiring ventilatory support. © 2015 Elsevier Inc.
